WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:07.200
Thank you to Curiosity Stream for supporting
PBS Digital Studios!

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.820
1,2,3, go!

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.379
Don’t rush me!

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.259
Philip, I promise I’ll catch you.

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:16.360
I know, but how do I know?

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.400
I’m your wife.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.140
Don’t you trust me?

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.140
Of course!

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.140
Then go for it!

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.140
Okay, okay!

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.140
Here we go…

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.510
Ah, I can’t!

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.200
Oh, forget it.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.120
No, no. I can do this.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.720
1...2...3…

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.760
Aaaah!

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.649
Trust is hard.

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:50.030
Counting on something you can’t see or touch
to be there for you when you need it can be

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.100
scary.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.890
But whether we like it or not, you and everyone
you know plays a global game of trust every

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:56.890
day.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.039
It’s called MONEY.

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:01.879
Money is imaginary.

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:05.540
This bill can’t be used as food or clothing
or shelter.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.550
And yet it has value because we’ve all agreed
to pretend that it does.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:15.270
The employee who accepts a paycheck for their
hours of work, the shopper who puts a new

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:20.190
pair of shoes on their credit card--even the
bankrobber risking his life for a bagful of

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:25.500
benjamins--they’re all putting their faith
in a collective fantasy that only works because

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.540
everyone else believes in it too.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.440
The earliest types of currency were things
that had practical value, like food or livestock

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:33.720
or furs.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.560
These commodity monies didn’t require much
trust.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.750
A toolmaker is willing to accept a bag of
barley as payment, because even if he has

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.510
more than he needs, he can be pretty sure
that someone else will want it.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:51.040
Because, you know, you can eat it!

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.340
But commodity money isn’t very convenient.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.090
Try schlepping forty bags of barley across
town just to pay your rent.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.320
And if your landlord doesn’t spend it right
away, it could mold or get eaten by rats.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.229
And if he wants to buy a tool from you, he
has to carry the sacks all the way back to

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:09.440
your house.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.129
At some point, someone realized that life
would be so much easier if instead of actually

00:02:14.129 --> 00:02:19.290
carrying sacks of barley back and forth, we
could just agree to pretend to carry sacks

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.640
of barley back and forth.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.629
We’d just need some way to keep track of
it all.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:29.349
This 5,000 year old Sumerian clay tablet is
one of the oldest examples of writing ever

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:30.349
found.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:34.870
It says: “29,086 measures of barley, 37
months.”

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:40.370
Writing wasn’t invented for royal decrees
or epic poems... it was invented to keep track

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.709
of imaginary sacks of barley--in other words,
credits and debits.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:49.310
So when St. Paul wrote that “love of money
is the root of all evil,” he may have had

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:53.490
a point, but he couldn’t have written it
down it if it weren’t for money.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:58.740
Once humans were willing to think about value
symbolically, it wasn’t long before we started

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.709
using other materials as currency.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:05.269
But of all the elements on the periodic scale,
which one should we use?

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:08.739
Well, first, it should be a solid.

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:10.670
And it can’t be toxic, obviously.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:17.130
It can’t be reactive.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.859
Or radioactive.

00:03:18.859 --> 00:03:21.310
It should be easy to extract and smelt.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.980
Rare enough to be valuable, but common enough
to be practical.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.279
It shouldn’t corrode, rust or degrade.

00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:30.450
But it should be soft enough to cut and shape.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.069
And… it should be pretty.

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:36.519
That really only leaves a few good options,
and the best fit is GOLD.

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:40.830
We’re used to thinking of gold as inherently
precious, but the main reason we use it as

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.230
a currency is… because it’s great at being
a currency.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.549
Physically, it’s not much more useful than
a paper bill.

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:48.549
Maybe less.

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:50.709
At least you can burn paper for warmth.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:55.900
So that paranoid survivalist burying bullion
in his backyard is still playing the trust

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:56.900
game.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.819
He’s trusting that even after the apocalypse,
everyone will agree to pretend that his gold

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:02.930
is valuable.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:10.150
The next big financial innovation came around
640 BCE in what is now Turkey, when someone

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:16.501
had the bright idea to pre-weigh lumps of
gold and stamp them with the royal seal, and

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:19.860
KA-CHING! the first coins were born.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.550
That stamp is the king’s personal guarantee
of the coin’s worth.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:30.130
No need to weigh it or test its purity--if
you trust the king, you can trust this coin.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:35.890
Counterfeiting is essentially royal impersonation--a
serious offense--and still known today as

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.290
a lese-majeste crime.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.290
Hm.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:39.290
Maybe that’s why the organization that investigates
counterfeiting in America is the same one

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:40.290
that protects the president from attacks:
the Secret Service.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.790
Coins were so convenient that they quickly
spread all over the world, and almost every

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:46.700
incarnation had some sort of symbol of trusted
authority on it.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.440
If you were lucky
enough to have a lot of these coins, you’d

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.060
need somewhere safe to store them, so BANKS
were founded where you could exchange your

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:01.180
gold coins for a slip of paper called a “promissory
note”: a “promise” that the bearer could

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.690
come back for their coins whenever they wanted.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:09.750
But a funny thing happened: these notes were
so light and convenient that people actually

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:15.480
preferred them as currency, and VOILA: Paper
money.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.350
Governments eventually took over the role
of printing banknotes, and covered them with

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:29.890
all types of assurances that this is more
than just a flat rectangle of cotton and linen.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.940
We especially love to put images of famous
people on our bills--not just to honor the

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.300
people, but to use their stature and respect
to reinforce trust in the currency.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.920
I mean, look at ol’ George there.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.250
Doesn’t he seem so TRUSTWORTHY?

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:45.670
“This money is REAL.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.900
I cannot tell a lie.”

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.760
So when you deposit your paycheck in the bank,
you’re taking it on faith that you’ll

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:58.650
be able to withdraw it later as cash, which
is a promise that can be redeemed for gold,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.880
which you’re hoping other people will exchange
for food and shelter and all the other stuff

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.400
we need to live.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.450
Wow, that’s some Inception levels of trust
going on there.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.580
Kinda scary when you think about it.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:16.880
Sure, but the fact that money is imaginary
does give you a lot more power in your relationship

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:17.880
with it.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.300
How you think about money affects its very
nature.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.190
In a future episode, we’ll look at how this
global trust experiment has moved beyond the

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:30.320
physical realm into the invisible world of
cyberspace.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.000
How can trust possibly survive when the majority
of the world’s wealth exists only in the

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.540
minds of computers?

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.780
We trust you’ll keep watching.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.920
